nvenc_drv.h (NVIDIA, SPDX MIT, "1993-2023") and clc5b7.h (NVIDIA, MIT): NVENC's picture-setup structures and class
methods from NVIDIA's open-gpu-doc (github.com/NVIDIA/open-gpu-doc, classes/video/, commit 9fdf5c40 2026-06-09).
Unmodified. nvenc_drv.h is written for class C9B7 but keeps one layout for every NVENC from MSENC on: its magic table
lists NV_NVENC_5_0 = 0xd0b70006 (T210's NVENC 5.0 firmware holds exactly that value) with the same revision byte as
every later class. The methods TegraNVENC uses have the same offsets in clc5b7.h, clc7b7.h and clc9b7.h. Struct sizes
match the header's own comments under clang (512-byte H.264 picture setup); test/nvenc_host checks them.
Used by TegraNVENCCheck.h and SwitchVideoEncoders/AVE.

Control values (SwitchVideoEncoders/AVE/H264NVENC.c, checked byte for byte by test/ave_host.c driverControls):
nvenc_drv.h documents the fields but not their values, and the first IDR sent with most of them 0 never finished (r7q,
TegraNVENCSelfTest.cpp step 3). The slice, ME, MD and quant controls, reference lists, slicing (slice_mode 0,
max_slice_size = macroblocks) and history size ((width_mbs * (height_mbs + 1)) * HIST_BLOCK_SIZE) are those of NVIDIA's
own T210 driver: its H.264 picture setups (magic 0xd0b70006, 1280x720 High profile CABAC 8x8) captured on a Switch and
published in github.com/TomasUribe/switch-frame-tap (GPL-2.0; data only, no code taken),
logs/m79-runE-setups/setup_3.bin (IDR; 8 and 9 identical) and setup_5.bin (P; 2, 4, 6, 7 identical), checked 2026-11-05,
decoded with this nvenc_drv.h. The two Ada NVENC hang causes found by github.com/mickys/nvenc (MIT)
docs/reports/T2.05e.md, slice_mode 1 and intra_ssd_cnt_* = 0, agree; NVENC 5.0's H.264 app does not read intra_ssd_cnt_*
(MD control bytes past 0x3a bits 0-2). What NVENC 5.0 reads was checked in the firmware (linux-firmware nvenc.bin,
envytools envydis -m falcon -V fuc5, the H.264 app at image offsets 0xc00-0xab00 = falcon addresses): setup at DMEM
0xa00 with the magic check at 0x8d96, ME control to DMEM 0x540 (read up to its mbc_mb_size word, written to I/O 0x1010
at 0x842a), MD to 0x600, quant to 0x1300, slice to 0x680 (per slice, 0x37c7-0x385a), hist_buf_size >> 7 to I/O 0xe0c
(0x3333).

End of an H.264 picture (tools/falcon, as SOURCE-H265): the app's ISR (0xf1b) adds I[0x1848] to DMEM 0x2e4 on each
encoder interrupt (IRQSTAT bit 13, I[0x1870] bit 1) and records errors in DMEM 0x2c8 (GP timer 0xe2640010, I[0x1870]
bit 0 0xe2640060, I[0x1ac4] bits 0-2 0xe2640200) without ending anything. fn 0x48b9 then spins at 0x4916-0x491b while
DMEM 0x2e4 < DMEM 0x354, with no timeout and no error check. DMEM 0x354 is the slice control's num_mb (0x38df),
clamped to the macroblocks left in the picture (0x38f7-0x390e) and given to the engine as I[0x191c] (0x39f5).

Picture size (tools/falcon flow_test): the app sizes the input fetch from input_cfg, (frame_*_minus1 + 1) >> 4
macroblocks, rounded down, into I[0xe88] (0x85d6-0x861b), and the picture from outputpic_cfg, (frame_*_minus1 + 16)
>> 4, rounded up, into DMEM 0x32c/0x32e, I[0x1808] (0x818c-0x81c3) and the slice target I[0x191c] (0x34d0). NVIDIA's
driver gives every surface the coded size (its 1920x1080 H.265 setup has frame_height_minus1 1087, SOURCE-H265), and
so does H264NVENC.c; the checker refuses any other. With 1080 the fetch stops a row short of the 68 macroblock rows the
engine encodes: the one slice never ends, the GP timer error lands with 0 of 8160 macroblocks counted (the ISR counts
when a slice ends, I[0x1870] bit 1 with the slice's bits in I[0x1ac4], 0xf6b-0xf8b) and the app spins at 0x4916 until
the kernel's job bound.

Where the OS keeps methods (tools/falcon on the OS overlay, 0x9f5-0xa51; the H.264 app reads them from DMEM 0x154, its
copy at 0x2e00): 0x700-0x74c are words 0-0x13 (0x780-0x7cc share them), with a written-bit mask at word 0x23 that
SET_APPLICATION_ID clears and the app's checks read (0x2f8d-0x3180); IN_REF_PIC0..15_LUMA are words 0x13-0x22. So on
NVENC 5.0 OUT_REF_PIC_CHROMA (0x74c) is reference slot 0, IN_REF_PIC0_CHROMA (0x440) overwrites the mask (E3's P
pictures' ucode error 0x120 on every boot of 11-06 is the app's 0xe2640120, CHROMA_V marked written but 0, which an
address bit in the mask gives), and 0x444-0x47c are dropped. The app
writes all 16 slots to I[0x1040-0x107c] on every picture (0x80a1, ME byte 0x8f bits 4-7 neither 1 nor 2, 0 here), and
NVIDIA's driver names slot 0 in every job it was captured sending (the other reconstruction), so H264NVENC.c's IDR does
too.
